// The xpbdsoftbody package: cloth, ropes, chains and inflated envelopes by
// extended position based dynamics (XPBD). A program reaches it with
// 'import xpbdsoftbody'.
//
// Uses in a scene: a flag in the wind, a hot-air balloon's envelope and its
// cables, curtains, a rope bridge, a lantern swinging on a chain, a cloak.
//
// Method: Macklin, Muller and Chentanez, "XPBD: Position-Based Simulation of
// Compliant Constrained Dynamics" (Motion in Games 2016) for the constraint
// solve, with Muller, Macklin, Chentanez, Jeschke and Kim, "Detailed Rigid
// Body Simulation with Extended Position Based Dynamics" (2020) for its way of
// stepping: many small substeps, one solve each, rather than many iterations
// of one big step. A compliance is the inverse of a stiffness, so a stiffness
// does not depend on the step or the iteration count, and 0 is a rigid
// constraint.
//
// - body.cone      SoftBody: particles (position, velocity, mass; pinned;
//                  attached to a moving frame), the constraint records, the
//                  colliders and the settings; Collider and Heightfield
// - solve.cone     the step, and the stretch, bending (dihedral), rope
//                  curvature, long range tether and pressure projections,
//                  and strain limiting
// - wind.cone      aerodynamic drag and lift on each triangle (and drag on a
//                  rope's segments), from a wind velocity the caller passes
// - collide.cone   contacts against spheres, capsules, boxes, planes, a
//                  heightfield and a caller's field closure, with friction;
//                  optional self collision in a hash grid
// - generate.cone  clothGrid, fromMesh (welded vertices, edges to constraints),
//                  rope, ropeBetween, chain, pressureBody, addTethers
// - output.cone    writeMesh and writeTube: positions and normals into a
//                  mesh.Mesh, to go to render's writeDynamicMesh
//
// Conventions are geomath's: right-handed, y up, metres, kilograms, seconds,
// f32. A cloth's front is the side its triangles run counterclockwise from.
//
//   mut flag = clothGrid(origin, across, down, 24u32, 16u32, &Fabric.light());
//   flag.pin(0u32);  flag.pin(...);               // the pole edge
//   flag.step(1. / 60., new Vec3(6., 0., 1.));      // each frame, wind a vector
//   writeMesh(&flag, &mut mesh, true, &mut scratch);
//
// Settings that matter (SoftBody's fields): 'substeps' (10 by default; the
// stretch left in a loaded cloth falls fast as it rises) and 'iterations' (1;
// a pressure body wants 2 or 3), 'strainLimit' (a hard cap on stretch, a
// fraction, for a cloth or rope that must not stretch), 'addTethers' (long
// range attachments, for hanging cloth), 'damping', 'friction', 'thickness',
// the wind coefficients and 'selfCollide'.
//
// What is not here: collisions between a cloth's triangles and a shape (only
// particles collide: the thickness is the margin), tearing, or the plasticity
// of a rope; self collision is particle against particle and only on request;
// the step is one thread's, in order, and not yet SIMD or coloured for threads.

mod xpbdsoftbody;

import libc;
import collections use *;
import geomath use *;
import mesh use Mesh;

fn fabs(a f32) f32 inline {libc.fabsf(a);}
fn fmin(a f32, b f32) f32 inline {if a < b {a;} else {b;};}
fn fmax(a f32, b f32) f32 inline {if a > b {a;} else {b;};}
fn fclamp(v f32, lo f32, hi f32) f32 inline {if v < lo {lo;} elif v > hi {hi;} else {v;};}
fn floorf(a f32) f32 inline {libc.floorf(a);}
